  "party": "Labour",
  "policy_title": "Support Small Businesses and Post Offices, and Address Horizon Scandal",
  "outcome": "O9",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O9",
    "direction": "improves",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "The policy's most direct relevance to O9 is its commitment to justice and compensation for sub-postmasters wrongly convicted under the Horizon scandal \u2014 a major due process failure. Significant progress has been made but parliamentary committees have identified serious structural failings and ongoing delays that undermine the 'swift' justice pledge.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the remaining compensation claims will be settled promptly and fully, and whether unsafe convictions linked to pre-Horizon systems like 'Capture' will be quashed \u2014 if these stall, the due-process improvement is partial at best.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy commits to ensuring swift justice and compensation for sub-postmasters affected by the Horizon IT scandal.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "ensure swift justice and compensation for sub-postmasters affected by the Horizon IT scandal"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "As of January 2026, approximately \u00a31.44 billion has been paid to over 11,300 claimants across the Horizon redress schemes.",
        "evidence_id": "E38",
        "quote": "approximately \u00a31.44 billion has been paid to over 11,300 claimants across the Horizon Shortfall Scheme (HSS), Group Litigation Order (GLO) scheme, and Horizon Convictions Redress Scheme (HCRS)"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Parliamentary committees have identified serious structural failings in the redress system, including unacceptable delays and processes that re-traumatise victims.",
        "evidence_id": "E40",
        "quote": "parliamentary committees, such as the Business and Trade Committee, have identified \"serious structural failings\" in the redress system, citing unacceptable delays, inadequate offers, and processes that \"re-traumatise\" victims"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "There are concerns about an unknown number of unsafe convictions linked to pre-Horizon systems, with calls for urgent legislation to quash them.",
        "evidence_id": "E42",
        "quote": "potentially unknown number of unsafe convictions, including those linked to pre-Horizon IT systems like \"Capture,\" with calls for urgent legislation to quash these"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The total cost of redress is estimated at around \u00a32 billion, and Fujitsu has not yet contributed despite acknowledging a moral obligation.",
        "evidence_id": "E41",
        "quote": "The total cost of redress is estimated to be around \u00a32 billion, yet Fujitsu, the system's provider, has not yet contributed to this bill, despite acknowledging a moral obligation"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "A new redress scheme for close family members affected by the scandal is anticipated to open in summer 2026.",
        "evidence_id": "E44",
        "quote": "A new redress scheme for close family members affected by the scandal is anticipated to open in summer 2026"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "O9 is concerned with due process, rule of law, and equal treatment. The Horizon IT scandal is one of the most significant due-process failures in modern UK history: hundreds of sub-postmasters were wrongly convicted, many losing livelihoods, liberty, and in some cases their lives. The policy's stated commitment to swift justice and compensation directly addresses a rule-of-law and equal-treatment deficit. The evidence shows real progress \u2014 \u00a31.44bn paid to over 11,300 claimants \u2014 which is a material, measurable improvement in the due-process position of a wronged minority. However, parliamentary committees have found serious structural failings: delays, inadequate offers, and processes that re-traumatise victims. There are also unresolved issues around unsafe convictions from pre-Horizon systems, and Fujitsu has not contributed financially despite a moral obligation. The remaining small-business and procurement elements of this policy are economic levers with no direct O9 mechanism; they do not affect equal treatment or due process in any traceable way and are therefore excluded from this verdict. On balance, the direction is 'improves' because the commitment to redress a major due-process injustice is real and partially delivered \u2014 but the magnitude is only moderate and confidence is moderate, because the 'swift justice' element is clearly incomplete and the structural failings are well-evidenced by credible parliamentary sources.",
